        "ai_analysis": {
          "root_cause": "The DUT failed bit-rate tolerance because its CAN bit time or phase margin could not remain synchronized with the EBS reference when the DUT bit rate was offset, causing bit decoding errors or bus-off. The empty numeric result table prevents quantifying the exact bit-time deviation, but the failure is consistent with oscillator/crystal tolerance, incorrect BRP/TSEG1/TSEG2/SJW configuration, or excessive propagation/phase error. The nine oscilloscope traces should be reviewed for sample-point drift, resynchronization phase error, and bus turnaround anomalies.",
          "iso_reference": "ISO 11898-1:2015 Clause 9 (Physical layer), Bit Rate Tolerance / Bit Time; project pass band 1998 ns to 2002 ns for 500 kbps",
          "severity": "MEDIUM",
          "recommended_fix": "1. Re-run PHL_TC006 with explicit +0.5% and -0.5% DUT bit-rate offsets and capture error counters, error frames, and bus-off status to identify the failing direction. 2. Verify the DUT CAN oscillator/crystal frequency, load capacitance, and bit timing registers (BRP, TSEG1, TSEG2, SJW) against the nominal 500 kbps bit time and ISO 11898-1 timing limits. 3. Analyze the nine oscilloscope traces for sample-point drift, resynchronization phase error, propagation delay, reflections, and bus turnaround anomalies. 4. Check harness length, 120 ohm termination, twisted-pair integrity, grounding, and connector integrity to rule out excessive propagation delay or reflections. 5. If oscillator drift or timing margin is confirmed, replace/upgrade the oscillator or adjust bit timing within ISO limits, then retest at nominal and offset bit rates."
        }
