        "ai_analysis": {
          "root_cause": "The PHL_TC_014 failure indicates the DUT/EBS CAN differential transition timing did not meet the ISO 11898-2 200 ns rise/fall time limit, although the result table did not capture numeric DUT/EBS values. This is typically caused by excessive bus capacitance, long stubs, improper 120-ohm termination, or transceiver slew-rate/noise issues that slow the dominant/recessive edge.",
          "iso_reference": "ISO 11898-2:2016, Clause 9.2.2 (Rise time tr and fall time tf; max 200 ns)",
          "severity": "HIGH",
          "recommended_fix": "1. Re-extract rise and fall times from the 9 captured oscilloscope traces using a differential probe on CAN_H/CAN_L at the DUT and EBS, trigger on dominant/recessive transitions, and populate the result table with measured ns values. 2. Verify bus topology by trimming CAN stubs to less than 0.3 m and reducing total bus capacitance from parallel loads, ESD devices, and connector pins. 3. Verify termination by confirming two 120-ohm +/-1% resistors at the bus extremes, measuring approximately 60-ohm differential bus impedance, and checking characteristic impedance near 120 ohms. 4. Inspect the transceiver output stage, slew-rate control pin configuration, common-mode choke, ESD protection, and ECU ground offsets; replace a faulty transceiver or ECU if timing remains out of spec. 5. Validate harness integrity, including twisted-pair continuity, shielding, connector impedance, separation from high-voltage inverter lines, and chassis ground bonding, then re-run PHL_TC_014."
        }
